# PromptCoach Reference

PromptCoach is the working program built from the **Learning to Prompt
framework**: a five-layer, evidence-graded design for software that teaches
middle school students to write prompts, specified in the MS thesis *Learning
to Prompt: A Design Framework for Middle School AI Literacy* (Panhapiseth Lim,
The University of Texas Permian Basin, 2026).

It ships with a **plug-in for AI assistants**: an MCP server (MCP is the open
standard that lets an assistant such as Claude, Gemini CLI, VS Code, or Cursor
call tools). With it, an assistant can look up the framework and check a tutor
design against it. See [Developer kit](#developer-kit-the-framework-for-agents-and-builders).

```bash
git clone https://github.com/BryanLim0214/promptcoach.git
cd promptcoach
python -m venv .venv
.venv/Scripts/python -m pip install -e ".[mcp]"      # .venv/bin/python on macOS/Linux
claude mcp add --transport stdio promptcoach -- "$(pwd)/.venv/Scripts/promptcoach-mcp"
```

Released under the MIT License (see `LICENSE`).

The framework was assembled after the studies: two user studies
(PicPrompt, an image-prompt game, and Promptémon, a prompt-selection game;
self-report outcomes) and three
coaching systems built without students (PromptAloud and Proem, MathEduAI,
PyPal). This repository is the reference implementation described in Chapter 11.

**What running it shows.** That the specification is implementable: each layer's
output is sufficient for the next layer's input, and the constraints the thesis
states — including a reasoned same-task revision, a calibration gap that belongs
to the student, a judge that is a different model from the one that answers
her, and scoring only the text she composed — can be enforced in code rather
than in prose.

**What it does not show.** Anything about students. It has not been used with
students. No student has used the framework, and none used the three builds it
draws on; the two user studies used earlier games and report self-report
outcomes. The four components original to the thesis (the taxonomy, the
progression, the reasoned revision and the calibration gap) are all Argued:
untested.

---

## Run it

```bash
# offline — no API key, no network
python -m venv .venv
.venv/Scripts/python -m pip install -e ".[dev]"   # .venv/bin/python on macOS/Linux
.venv/Scripts/python -m pytest                    # 372 tests
.venv/Scripts/python -m promptcoach_ref.demo      # the worked example, in the terminal

# the app
cd web && npm install && npm run build && cd ..
.venv/Scripts/python -m uvicorn promptcoach_ref.api:app --port 8823
# http://127.0.0.1:8823            the student app
# http://127.0.0.1:8823/inspect    the researcher view
```

Use the project venv: a global FastAPI/Starlette mismatch on some machines fails
at import.

With a key, the layers route to real models:

```bash
export GEMINI_API_KEY=...               # or OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...
export PROMPTCOACH_GENERATOR=...        # optional: the model that answers her prompt
export PROMPTCOACH_CROSS_MODEL=1        # optional: a second scorer
```

To recompute ICC and paraphrase shift with live models, `eval.revalidate` needs
`OPENROUTER_API_KEY` (without it, the script runs its offline protocol check):

```bash
export OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...
python -m eval.revalidate
```

Frontend dev server with hot reload: `cd web && npm run dev` (proxies `/api` to
port 8823).

---

## Developer kit: the framework for agents and builders

For people building AI tutors that teach prompt writing, the framework is also
available as tools an AI agent can call:

| | |
|---|---|
| `promptcoach-mcp` | An MCP server (stdio, offline, read-only, no key): the 24 components with grades and thesis sections, the rubric, taxonomy, help ladder, bands, commitments, a 24-question design checklist, `audit_tutor_design`, and an offline `coach_attempt` demo |
| `DEVELOPER_GUIDE.md` | Do and do-not rules for each layer, with evidence grades, client setup, and student-data cautions |
| `skills/prompt-tutor-design-review/` | An Agent Skill that reviews a tutor design with or without the server |
| `AGENTS.md` | Rules for coding agents working in this repository |

```bash
.venv/Scripts/python -m pip install -e ".[mcp]"
claude mcp add --transport stdio promptcoach -- <path>/.venv/Scripts/promptcoach-mcp
```

The server only reads `promptcoach_ref/framework`, so it states what the thesis
states; `tests/test_devkit.py` checks that it never promotes a grade or claims
learning. The audit reports coverage against the framework, not quality.

---

## The idea that shapes the codebase

The thesis specifies output contracts for Layers 1–4 and **twenty-four other
things not at all**: per-layer inputs, Layer 5's output, any state machine, the
confidence formula, sentinel aggregation and threshold, the classifier, the
ranked-list length, escalation thresholds, de-escalation, the stop condition,
gating values, the decomposition rules, the task bank, band placement, response
templates, the worked-contrast generator — and, new with the redesigned
framework, the minimum length of a revision reason, when the revision hold lets
go, whether she sees her calibration gap, and which model generates the answer —
and, after the second review round, what counts as an unchanged prompt for the
evaluative escalation rule and what counts as a success for the rate and streak.

Builds 2 and 3 supply concrete numbers — 85% + 3 correct, ≤200 characters, 3
errors — but the thesis marks every one as set *for a task with a correct
answer*. So the risk is invention laundered as specification. Two packages
answer it:

| | |
|---|---|
| `promptcoach_ref/framework/` | Only what the thesis states. Every constant carries a citation to a **section label** of the thesis (`sec:calibrate`, `sec:promptemon`, …), never a file or a line, and every graded component carries a mechanism grade **and** a need basis. No network, no I/O, no invented values. |
| `promptcoach_ref/policy/` | Every value the thesis leaves open. Each carries `thesis_says` (the passage proving it is open), `borrowed_from`, `caveat` and `status`. |

`framework` never imports `policy`, and a test enforces it. A `Cite` naming
anything other than a label from the chapter map cannot be constructed, and the
traceability generator checks every cited label against `\label{...}` in the
chapter sources when they are on disk.

### Two grade columns

| Column | Values | Counts (pinned by `test_grades.py`) |
|---|---|---|
| Mechanism grade | Direct / Transferred / Argued | 4 / 10 / 10 |
| Need basis | User studies / Builds / Argument | 4 / 18 / 2 |

24 components. The four Direct rows are Build 1 measurement results on
benchmarks of 30 items or fewer. A visible consequence before coaching is
Argued: both games showed students the output first and students accepted it
(self-report), but the order was never varied, so the mechanism is untested.

### What changed with the redesign

- **Affective state inference is gone.** This implementation had already switched
  it off, because a long pause before a prompt may be planning rather than
  struggle; the thesis then removed it from the framework (`sec:not-adopted`).
  `policy/affect.py` keeps the note that it was tried and removed.
- **Escalation no longer reads inactivity.** It escalates on repeated failed
  attempts and explicit requests for help. When the diagnosis is paired with
  Iteration Awareness or Reflection (blind acceptance, undiagnosed retry,
  delegated judgment), the repeated-attempt count is not used: help moves up
  only when she asks or when her new attempt leaves the prompt text unchanged
  (`escalation.evaluative_pairing`).
- **Help climbs across revisions of one task** (third review round, E01). A
  help cycle is the help given on one task for one top-ranked category
  (`escalation.cycle`). Each revision is first measured by Layer 5 against the
  attempt it revises. While the same category is still ranked first, the
  paired dimension has not improved and level 4 has not been issued, the cycle
  stays open and Layer 4 answers the revision. Help moves up one rung when the
  same category is ranked first on two consecutive attempts, first attempts and
  revisions alike (`escalation.consecutive_unresolved` = 2), or when she presses
  **I need more help** (`asked_for_help`). So a first attempt and three
  revisions with the same unresolved problem get levels 1, 2, 3 and 4, the
  last being the worked contrast. The cycle closes when the paired dimension
  improves, when the top category changes, or on the attempt after level 4,
  which is recorded as unresolved instead of repeating level 4. A revision
  never opens a new cycle unless she asks for more help. Before this change a
  revision always closed the cycle, so help never rose above level 1 in the
  student's session.
- **The revision hold and the ladder together.** Her first reasoned revision
  releases the hold even while the help cycle is still open; she may move on
  (which starts the next task at level 1) or keep revising (help keeps
  climbing). An unreasoned revision stops at Layer 1, so it neither raises nor
  resets help, but it counts toward the four attempts after which the hold
  releases by itself.
- **What a success is, for difficulty gating.** An attempt counts as a success
  when the dimensions its band requires score 3 or higher: every named planning
  dimension in bands 1–2, three of the four planning dimensions in band 3, one
  of the two evaluative dimensions in bands 4–5 (`gating.success`). One outcome
  is recorded each time Layer 5 runs (every scored revision, and a first
  attempt with nothing to diagnose), so the window and streak count attempts and
  revisions, not distinct tasks (`gating.streak_unit`); with two tasks per band
  in this placeholder bank, a streak of three needs a revised or repeated task.
- **The revision hold lets go by itself.** After four attempts on a task the
  hold releases automatically, and each release without a reasoned revision is
  recorded in `Session.gate_exits` (for F7). No route or setting releases it
  by hand.
- **Five new components**, each enforced in code:

| Component | Grade / need | Where |
|---|---|---|
| Visible consequence before coaching | Argued / User studies | `session.py` runs her prompt before any coaching |
| Same-task revision with a stated reason | Argued / User studies | `framework/revision.py`, `layers/elicit.py`; switch in `policy/revision.py` |
| Predicted rating beside the measured score | Argued / User studies | `contracts.calibration_gap`, `layers/calibrate.py`; display switch in `policy/calibration.py` |
| Judge independent of the generating model | Transferred / Argument | generator is a different model from every judge (`policy/generator.py`); `layers/measure.py` records both |
| Selecting is not composing | Argued / User studies | `inserted_spans`; `layers/measure.py` scores only what she composed |

`framework/classroom.py` holds the classroom aggregates — Promptémon from the raw
re-analysis only, PicPrompt from the published article only — each with its
section label, and a comparability flag per pre/post item pair. A pair whose two
items were worded differently can never be presented as change.

---

## The five layers

```
Elicit ──┬─→ Measure ──→ Diagnose ──→ Respond ──→ Calibrate
         └──────────────────↗ ↗ ↗ ↗
```

Not a pipeline. *"The dependency is not a single chain: Layer 3 reads Layer 1's
paired record and the session history directly, because four of its seven
categories are invisible in the prompt text that Layer 2 scores."*

Around it runs the student's own attempt cycle: **Plan → Write → Predict** (she
rates the prompt 1 to 4, optional) **→ See** (the prompt runs and she reads what
it produced) **→ Revise** (the same task, with a short stated reason).

| Layer | Requirement passed downstream | What the redesign added |
|---|---|---|
| 1 Elicit | "a paired record of intent and artifact, plus timing" | predicted rating; asks for a reason when a revision has none; records inserted spans |
| 2 Measure | "six dimension scores with per dimension confidence" | scores only what she composed; records judge and generator |
| 3 Diagnose | "a ranked list of failure categories with confidence" | reads her composed text |
| 4 Respond | "the level issued, and whether the next attempt improved…" | treats inserted text as not hers |
| 5 Calibrate | *— the thesis states none* | calibration gap; whether a reasoned revision is pending |

Layer 5's output shape is ours, and `CalibrateOutput.THESIS_SPECIFIED` is `False`
to say so.

**Which over-reliance detector runs.** In a live session Layer 5 calls the
judge engine's model-based detector (`GeminiEngine.sentinel`), which is the
detector `eval/benchmark.py` measured and the replication AUC describes. The
keyword rules (`DeterministicEngine.sentinel`) run only in the offline engine:
they are an **author-tested stand-in**: scored once against the risk labels on the
authors' own 30 items (AUC 0.847, `eval/offline_sentinel.py`), so not an independent test.
Every Layer 5 result names its detector in `sentinel_source`. If the detector
call fails, or the engine has none (OpenRouter), no signals are shown; the
keyword rules are never substituted for the model. **Layer 3 does not read**
the sentinel: intent drift, undiagnosed retry, delegated judgment and borrowed
phrasing are detected by Layer 3's own rules over the plan, the prompt and the
session history (offline, the rule set; live, the judge model's diagnosis call).

**Help already given is on the record.** Each attempt carries the help
response it followed (`triggering_intervention_id`), so Layer 3 can see that
help was offered before an unchanged resubmission. Without it, blind
acceptance could never fire through `Session.submit`.

**The rule the whole of Layer 4 exists to enforce:** *"no level supplies a
missing element on her behalf. Guidance may narrow the search space and may not
close it."* `layers/respond.py::validate` is that sentence as code.

---

## Model routing

Two settings, never one. The **judge** scores her prompt, diagnoses it and
composes the coaching; the **generator** answers her prompt. They are configured
separately so the model whose answer she reads is a different model from the one
that scores her prompt. The requirement is a different *model*, not a different
vendor: on the Gemini provider the scorer, the comparator and the generator are
all Google models. Only the OpenRouter defaults put the three on different
vendors (Anthropic, Google, OpenAI).

| Role | Gemini | OpenRouter | Setting |
|---|---|---|---|
| Judge (scorer) | `gemini-3.1-flash-lite` | `anthropic/claude-opus-5` | `PROMPTCOACH_SCORER` (Gemini) |
| Generator | `gemini-3.1-pro-preview` | `openai/gpt-5.4-mini` | `PROMPTCOACH_GENERATOR` |
| Cross-model comparator | `gemini-3.8-flash` | `google/gemini-3.8-flash` | `PROMPTCOACH_CROSS_MODEL=1` |

In every shipped configuration the generator is a third model, neither the
scorer nor the comparator, with or without `PROMPTCOACH_CROSS_MODEL=1`. A live
configuration whose generator equals a judge **refuses to start** with a message
naming the collision; it never runs non-independent. Every Measure result also
records `judge_model`, `generator_model` and `judge_independent_of_generator`.
Offline, the rule engine judges and a scripted responder answers, unaffected by
the check. (The Gemini generator is the Pro preview, which was slow in informal
testing; it answers once per attempt and is off the scoring path.)

Reliability ships as a **command, not a constant**: a coefficient tied to two
model versions expires when either is retired.

---

## Frontend

Vite + React + TypeScript, in `web/`.

| | |
|---|---|
| Behaviour & accessibility | `radix-ui` — unstyled primitives. Accessibility is a framework commitment with a rejection test, not a polish item. |
| Charts | `d3-scale` only. The meters are bespoke SVG. |
| Type | Newsreader (the student's speech), IBM Plex Sans (chrome), IBM Plex Mono (the prompt artifact) — vendored via `@fontsource`, so it builds with no network. |

The student app (`/`) shows a task, her prompt's answer, and one question. It now
also carries a **1–4 confidence control** before she sends, a **reason field** on
a revision, a line naming **the model that wrote the answer** (and that a
different model scored the prompt), and — when the policy shows it — her guess
beside the score, which only she sees. Under each piece of coaching there is an
**I need more help** control; her next try is sent with `asked_for_help`, and
the help on it moves up one rung.

The researcher view (`/inspect`): **Session** (the loop), **Trace** (every layer
opened, with its contract sentence beside the value the code produced),
**Instrument** (the 24 components with both grade columns, and the twenty-four
gaps), **About** (thesis statement, research questions, requirements R1–R6,
threats, remaining work).

---

## Tests

372, hermetic, no network (356 for the framework and app, 16 for the developer kit). Each tier is a `pytest` marker.

| Marker | What it holds |
|---|---|
| `contract` | Each layer takes only its input, emits only its output; the new fields (predicted rating, reason, inserted spans, judge/generator, calibration gap, revision pending) exist and behave. |
| `rejection` | The prohibitions: no level supplies a missing element; the sentinel and calibration data never reach a teacher view; a revision without a reason is not scored; inserted text is not scored as hers; the generator differs from every judge in every shipped live configuration, and a colliding configuration refuses to start; withdrawn figures appear nowhere in the source; audio is never stored; a scoring failure is never a zero; gamification is absent. |
| `mechanism` | The dual gate refuses to move on rate alone *or* streak alone; the ladder never skips; inactivity and latency-based affect are removed; a first attempt and three revisions reach level 4 through `Session.submit`, an evaluative diagnosis stays put unless she asks, and the revision hold and the ladder work together (`test_review_r3.py`). |
| `grade` | 24 components; counts pinned at 4 / 10 / 10 and 4 / 18 / 2; every citation is a section label (checked against the chapter sources when present). |
| `falsifiability` | The implementation keeps what its own refutation needs: unpaired deltas (F2), both selection modes (F4), the reason requirement as a switch (F7), the gap display as a switch with the gap still recorded (F8), judge and generator on every attempt (F9). |
| `classroom` | Aggregates pinned to the raw re-analysis and the published PicPrompt numbers; non-comparable pairs can never be presented as change. |
| `devkit` | The design checklist covers all 24 components without promoting a grade; the audit never claims quality or learning; every MCP tool is read-only and closed-world; the server answers over real stdio. |

Plus `test_worked_example.py`, the golden transcript: the thesis's worked example
replayed, asserting the thesis's own claims about it.

```bash
.venv/Scripts/python -m pytest -m falsifiability -v
```

---

## Traceability

`TRACEABILITY.md` is generated, never hand-edited:

```bash
.venv/Scripts/python -m promptcoach_ref.traceability
```

It maps all thirteen chapters to module and test, checks every cited section
label, lists the twenty-four graded components with both columns, the classroom
item pairs with their comparability, the twenty-four gaps with what was chosen
instead, and names what is **not implementable** and why — the teacher-agreement
bar, classroom feasibility of speech capture, taxonomy coverage, the band
ordering, whether the reasoned revision or the visible gap does anything, and
any instructional effect at all.

That last list is the point of the document. A reference implementation that
quietly skipped the things it could not do would be a worse baseline than one
that names them.
